  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#beyond"/>
    <link type="seealso" xref="programs"/>
    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <desc>The terminal, KSM, the folders of the programs, and the other
    settings.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>Settings</title>
  <p>Choose <guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Settings</gui></guiseq>, or press
  <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>P</key></keyseq>. The window has a page for each
  place where settings are kept, and a line at the top of each page says
  where. Each setting has a line that says what it does.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The Settings window</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/settings.png">
      <p>The Settings window, on its page This computer: Terminal, Tray icon,
      Missing programs, Share memory (KSM), Audio driver and Workspace.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <p>The windows of another Virtualbricks, opened with
  <cmd>virtualbricks --connect</cmd>, have a third page, for the computer
  where the bricks run; see <link xref="remote"/>.</p>
  <p if:test="!target:html">Every setting of the files is in
  <link href="man:virtualbricks-config(5)">virtualbricks-config(5)</link>.</p>
  <p if:test="target:html">Every setting of the files is in
  <link href="https://www.virtualbricks.it/documentation/config-files.html">virtualbricks-config(5)</link>.</p>
  <section id="computer">
    <title>This computer</title>
    <p>The settings of this computer, for every project, in
    <file>~/.config/virtualbricks/settings.toml</file>:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Terminal</gui></title>
        <p>The terminal that opens the consoles of the bricks. The menu offers
        those of this computer that can.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Tray icon</gui></title>
        <p>An icon in the system tray.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Missing programs</gui></title>
        <p>A warning at start about the programs Virtualbricks can't find.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Share memory (KSM)</gui></title>
        <p>Share equal memory pages between machines, with the KSM of Linux.
        It turns on or off when you click <gui>OK</gui>, through
        <cmd>sudo</cmd>; if it can't, the window stays open on it, and says
        why.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Audio driver</gui></title>
        <p>The driver that QEMU plays the sound cards through, as
        <sys>alsa</sys>, <sys>pa</sys> or <sys>pipewire</sys>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Workspace</gui></title>
        <p>The folder of the projects; see <link xref="projects#workspace"/>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="project">
    <title>The project</title>
    <p>The settings of the open project, in its <file>project.toml</file>, so
    that it runs the same on another computer. With no project open, the page
    is greyed.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>QEMU folder</gui>, <gui>VDE folder</gui></title>
        <p>Where the programs are; under each, what the folder holds. See
        <link xref="programs#where"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Plugs into machines</gui></title>
        <p>Let plugs go into the socket cards of machines.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Loops of links</gui></title>
        <p>Log an error when links make a loop.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="ok">
    <title>OK and Cancel</title>
    <p><gui>OK</gui> writes only what you changed, so a setting changed
    meanwhile from the console stays. A setting that is wrong marks its page,
    and <gui>OK</gui> stays greyed, with the reason in its tooltip.
    <gui>Cancel</gui> changes nothing.</p>
  </section>
</page>
